{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>Better a little with fear of HaShem than a great treasure and turmoil in it; better a meal of herbs with love there than a stalled ox with hatred in it</b> (Proverbs 15:16)." "<b>Solomon</b> the king, peace be upon him, cited these two verses (Proverbs 15) concerning the trait of sufficiency, and warned in them against robbery. And they may be interpreted in three ways: peshat, Midrash, and reason.\n\nBy way of the plain sense (peshat): 'Better a little with fear of HaShem' — Solomon, peace be upon him, warns a person concerning the matter of robbery, for it is a rational commandment among the rational commandments. For anyone who has the sin of robbery upon him, it is harsher than all other sins; and so our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'A seah full of sins — none of them prosecutes except robbery'; and the generation of the Flood was not sentenced except for robbery, as it says (Genesis 6:13): ״קץ כל בשר בא לפני כי מלאה הארץ חמס״. And if one does not return the robbery, there is no forgiveness ever; therefore it is a transgression more severe than all the rest. Therefore Solomon, peace be upon him, said: 'Better a little with fear of HaShem' — that is, better is the little, which is what remains of the much, and which is the forbidden [i.e., wealth gained by robbery]; and so he said nearby (Proverbs 16:8): ״טוב מעט בצדקה מרוב תבואות בלא משפט״, coming to warn a person about robbery, that he should not be sparing of his silver and gold, saying: how could he distance it from between his hands? For better is a little that remains to him in righteousness than abundant produce without justice. And concerning this he said: 'Better a little with fear of HaShem' and in His love, than a multiplicity of treasures and storehouses of silver and gold and turmoil within them.\n\nAnd he brought as evidence for this the second verse that follows it: ״טוב ארוחת ירק ואהבה שם״ — for after he warned about robbery, which is a rational matter, he brought a natural parable for proof: for it is the nature of human beings to prefer a light and meager meal, such as a meal of herbs and the like, in the company of friends, over fattened oxen in the company of enemies. From this, nature can understand and reason and draw a kal va-ḥomer from what is well-known and perceived by the senses: if in love of flesh and blood a person chooses and finds pleasing a light meal more than a banquet of delicacies and rich foods in an atmosphere of hatred — how much more so is it fitting for him to choose 'a little with fear of HaShem' and His love, which is better for him than any love of any person and than any multiplicity of treasures and all the money in the world.\n\nAnd this entire matter is a warning and an admonition and a way of life: that a person should not train himself in the desire for money, and should not make his silver and gold the foundation of his faith, and his wealth and property the fortress of his strength. This is what Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 18:11): ״הון עשיר קרית עזו וכחומה נשגבה במשכיתו״. Scripture says: just as it is human nature to place one's trust in a fortified city that has a wall and a tower, so does the wealthy person place his trust in his wealth. And this verse is a warning to a person to distance himself from this trait — that a person should not trust in his riches nor make them his fortified city, but rather let the Name of HaShem alone be his wealth and his fortified city. And it is linked with the verse above it: ״מגדל עוז שם ה' בו ירוץ צדיק ונשגב״, followed by ״הון עשיר קרית עזו״ — the intention is that the Name of HaShem is the wealth of the rich, for the righteous place their silver and gold in the service of Torah and commandments, while the wicked place Torah and commandments in the service of their silver and gold. Therefore it is not fitting for a person to place his trust and hope in anything but the unique Name.\n\nAnd this matter Eliphaz the Temanite explained to Job, as he said (Job 22:24): ״ושית על עפר בצר ובצור נחלים אופיר״. He said to Job: It is fitting for you to set money in your mind at the level of dust, and to set the gold of Ophir like a rock of riverbeds, in which a person should not trust, for there is no benefit in it. And after he mentioned that one should not place trust and hope in one's wealth, he returned to warn him to place his trust in HaShem, may He be exalted, as it is written (ibid.): ״והיה שדי בצריך וכסף תועפות לך״, and it is written (ibid.): ״כי אז על שדי תתענג תשא אל אלוה פניך״." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'Better a little with fear of HaShem' — better are Israel before the Holy One, blessed be He, for it is said of them (Deuteronomy 7:7): ״כי אתם המעט מכל העמים״. 'With fear of HaShem' — that they accepted the Torah in order to fear HaShem, may He be exalted, as it says (Exodus 20:17): ״כי לבעבור נסות אתכם בא האלהים ובעבור תהיה יראתו על פניכם לבלתי תחטאו״. 'Than a great treasure' — these are the seventy nations, which are compared to a treasure of wine, as our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Song of Songs 2:4): ״הביאני אל בית היין״ — these are the seventy idolatrous nations. 'And turmoil in it' — that the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to overwhelm them and punish them on the Day of Judgment. 'Better a meal of herbs with love there' — better is the meal of herbs that Solomon the king ate with a poor man than a stalled ox that he ate with a rich man." "<b>They said</b> in the Midrash: At the time that Solomon descended from his kingship, he would go about from door to door and say (Ecclesiastes 1): ״אני קהלת הייתי מלך על ישראל בירושלים״. Two men who recognized him encountered him. One of them prostrated himself before him and said: 'My lord the king, if you wish, accompany me today.' He went with him and brought him up to his upper room. He brought him a stalled ox and prepared for him several delicacies and set them before him, and reminded him of the affairs of his kingship. He said to him: 'Remember when you were king, you did such and such on such and such a day.' When he reminded him of his kingship, he burst into weeping and did not eat of that entire meal, and did not cease weeping until he rose from that meal hungry, in weeping. The second one encountered him, prostrated himself before him, and said: 'My lord the king, would you like to accompany me today?' He said to him: 'Do you perhaps wish to do for me as your fellow did yesterday?' He said to him: 'I am a poor man; if you wish, accompany me today for a little greens that I have.' He went to his house. When he arrived there, he washed his hands and feet for him and brought before him a little greens. He began to comfort him and said: 'My lord the king, an oath the Holy One, blessed be He, swore to David your father, that the kingship shall never cease from his seed, as it says (Psalms 132): ״נשבע ה' לדוד אמת לא ישוב ממנה״. But the trait of the Holy One, blessed be He, is thus: He rebukes a person and then returns and is reconciled, as it says (Proverbs 3): ״כי את אשר יאהב ה' יוכיח וכאב את בן ירצה״. And the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to restore you to your kingship.' When Solomon the king heard this, his mind was eased and he rejoiced in that meal of herbs, and he rose from there full and rejoicing and glad of heart. When he returned to his kingship, he wrote in his wisdom: ״טוב ארוחת ירק ואהבה שם משור אבוס ושנאה בו״ — better was the meal of herbs that the poor man fed me than the stalled ox that the rich man fed me, who reminded me of my suffering." "<b>And by way of reason (sekhel):</b> 'Better a little with fear of HaShem' — Solomon warns a person to contemplate intellectual matters according to his grasp in the realm of the possible, and not to desire to be too clever and to trouble his intellect with the realm of the impossible, concerning which he said (Ecclesiastes 7:16): ״ואל תתחכם יותר״ — 'for better is a little with fear of HaShem.' That is, it is better that he understand and attain a small apprehension and fear HaShem, blessed be He. 'Than a great treasure' — that is, than a multiplicity of wisdom beyond his capacity to grasp, for from this will come confusion and turmoil, and this is 'and turmoil in it.' For with the little, you will be elevated and delight, and with the much, you will be lost and bewildered, and your soul will be driven away from the true eternal life — from those ranks that are fitting to be given to it from the King's palace. It is like the matter of honey, which is sweet to the body in small measure but whose abundance harms it; and concerning this Solomon said (Proverbs 25:16): ״דבש מצאת אכל דיך פן תשבענו והקאותו״ — he warns to eat from it only enough for his fill and leave what remains within, for if he eats from it in abundance and excess beyond his need, he will vomit it all, and even the little he had already acquired he will lose. So too will it happen to one who thinks to apprehend more than his measure and beyond his capacity: when he wishes to go forward, he will in any case fall backward.\n\nAnd our Rabbis of blessed memory explained to us a parable concerning this matter — what they said in the chapter 'Ḥelek' regarding Balaam, who went to Midian to take wages for the twenty-four thousand [Israelites] he caused to fall from Israel: 'A camel came to ask for horns; they cut off his ears from him,' as it says (Joshua 13:22): ״ואת בלעם בן בעור הקוסם הרגו בחרב״ — that is, he sought to take what was not his, and what was his he lost. And what our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted concerning wisdom: ״דבש מצאת אכול דיך״ — this is Rabbi Akiva; ״פן תשבענו והקאותו״ — this is Ben Zoma." "<b>After</b> he instructed to gain understanding and to investigate the domain of the possible and warned him not to investigate the domain of the impossible, he comes to speak of the domain of apprehension and warns about it that his apprehension should be refined and distilled, without dross. This is what he said: ״A meal of herbs with love there״ (Proverbs 15:17) — he said: it is good to attain a light apprehension together with faith in the Shekhinah, which is called love, as it is said (Song of Songs 2:7): ״If you awaken, if you arouse the love until it pleases,״ and it is written (ibid. 8:7): ״If a man give all the wealth of his house for love.״ And we say: ״In love You have raised our horn, to give thanks to You and to declare Your oneness״ — from ״an ox from the stall,״ meaning from the great apprehension that leads a person astray, like that apprehension which Israel attained in the wilderness, when they made the calf and exchanged their glory for the likeness of an ox (שור). And this hatred is the attribute of judgment; this is what Moses mentioned (Exodus 32:11): ״Why, HaShem, does Your anger burn against Your people?״ And the general sense of the verse according to this approach is that he praises the service of the Shekhinah and condemns that sin. And since their intention was not idolatry but rather they sought it as a leader, therefore the sin was forgiven them. And the proof that it was forgiven them is that immediately the Shekhinah dwelt in the Mishkan, for this was a sign and testimony that the sin was forgiven them. Therefore the Tent of Meeting in the wilderness is called Mishkan, from the language of Shekhinah, and the Mishkan is called משכן העדות — the Mishkan of testimony. And our Sages of blessed memory interpreted: they sinned in these and were appeased in these — they sinned in these (Exodus 32:4, 8): ״אלה אלהיך ישראל״ — 'these are your gods, O Israel,' and they were appeased in these (Exodus 38:21): ״אלה פקודי המשכן״ — 'these are the reckonings of the Mishkan.'" "<b>These are the reckonings of the Mishkan, the Mishkan of testimony, which were reckoned at the mouth of Moses, the service of the Levites, by the hand of Itamar son of Aaron the priest.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): 'These are the reckonings of the Mishkan' refers back to the items mentioned above — he says that the Mishkan and its vessels, which are the structure and the courtyard and all made for them, are 'the reckonings of the service of the Levites, which Moses reckoned by the hand of Itamar.' But the holy vessels, which are the Ark, the Table, the Menorah, and the Altars, are not included in the Mishkan; they are the charge of Eleazar." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'These are the reckonings of the Mishkan — Mishkan.' Why is 'Mishkan' stated twice? Rather, to teach that the sanctuary below corresponds to the sanctuary above, as it is said (Exodus 15:17): ״The foundation of Your dwelling You made, HaShem,״ etc. — do not read מכון (foundation) but rather מכוון (correspondence). The Mishkan was equivalent to the creation of the world. In the creation of the world it is written (Isaiah 40:22): ״Who stretches out the heavens like a gauze and spreads them,״ and regarding the Mishkan it is written (Exodus 26:7): ״And you shall make curtains of goats' hair for a tent,״ and it is written (Psalms 104:2): ״Who stretches out the heavens like a curtain.״ In the creation of the world it is written (Genesis 1:9): ״Let the waters be gathered,״ and regarding the Mishkan (Exodus 30:18): ״And you shall make a basin of bronze.״ In the creation of the world it is written (Genesis 1:14): ״Let there be lights,״ and regarding the Mishkan (Exodus 25:31): ״And you shall make a Menorah.״ In the creation of the world it is written (Genesis 1:20): ״And birds shall fly,״ and regarding the Mishkan (Exodus 25:20): ״And the cherubim shall spread their wings.״ In the creation of the world it is written (Genesis 1:27): ״And Elohim created the human,״ and regarding the Mishkan (Exodus 28:1): ״And you, bring near to yourself.״ In the creation of the world it is written (Genesis 2:1): ״And the heavens were finished,״ and regarding the Mishkan (Exodus 39:32): ״And all the service was finished.״ In the creation of the world it is written (Genesis 2:3): ״And Elohim blessed the seventh day and sanctified it,״ and regarding the Mishkan (Exodus 39:43): ״And Moses blessed them,״ and it is written (Numbers 7:1): ״And he sanctified it and all its vessels.״ In the creation of the world it is written (Genesis 2:3): ״For in it He rested,״ and regarding the Mishkan (Exodus 35:2): ״Six days shall work be done.״ And afterward He said: ״Take from among you a contribution,״ — to inform you that the work of the Mishkan was equivalent to the heavens and the earth. Thus far in the Midrash." "<b>All</b> that the Sages mentioned in this Midrash regarding matters of the Mishkan corresponding to the world — it appears they spoke in general terms, but the particulars are many and are drawn out and derived from the general principle. And it appears to me that from this general principle we have learned, we can innovate and derive some particulars, namely that I found among these correspondences: just as it is written in the creation of the world (Genesis 2:4): ״אלה תולדות השמים והארץ״ — 'these are the generations of the heavens and the earth,' correspondingly we find regarding the Mishkan: ״אלה פקודי המשכן״ — 'these are the reckonings of the Mishkan.' And just as the world was created with the letter heh, as it is stated (Genesis 2:4): ״בהבראם״ — and our Sages of blessed memory interpreted: 'with a heh He created them,' correspondingly we find regarding the Mishkan (Exodus 40:34): ״וכבוד ה׳ מלא את המשכן״ — 'and the glory of HaShem filled the Mishkan,' for the heh is the glory of HaShem. And this is the intention, as the matter of the Mishkan is mentioned many times in the Torah, as I wrote at the beginning of Parashat Vayakhel. And likewise, the language of Mishkan is the language of Shekhinah, and this is what is written (Psalms 85:10): ״לשכון כבוד בארצנו״ — 'that glory may dwell in our land.' And just as the world arose in thought to be created first, and afterward the acts were performed in six days, correspondingly you find in the Mishkan: first to think thoughts and afterward to do. And just as the world was created not as an obligation but by way of freewill offering and kindness, as it is stated (Psalms 89:3): ״עולם חסד יבנה״ — 'the world is built with kindness,' so the Mishkan was built by way of freewill offering, as it is stated (Exodus 25:2): ״אשר ידבנו לבו״ — 'whose heart moves him,' (ibid. 35): ״כל נדיב לבו יביאה״ — 'everyone whose heart is generous shall bring it.' And just as there He mentioned Elohim in every act of Genesis and afterward the proper Name, as it is stated (Genesis 2:4): ״ביום עשות ה׳ אלהים,״ correspondingly you find in the Mishkan: after He mentioned (Exodus 40:34) ״וכבוד ה׳ מלא את המשכן״ — 'and the glory of HaShem filled the Mishkan,' which corresponds to the name Elohim, He mentioned the proper Name HaShem and said (ibid.): ״כי ענן ה׳ על המשכן״ — 'for the cloud of HaShem was upon the Mishkan.' And just as the world was created with two attributes — the attribute of mercy and the attribute of judgment — correspondingly the Mishkan was made with two craftsmen: Bezalel from the tribe of Judah, corresponding to the proper Name, and Oholiab from the tribe of Dan, corresponding to the attribute of judgment." "<b>And also,</b> it is possible that we may understand the word Mishkan from the language of drawing down (המשכה), for the Mishkan and its vessels are figures and likenesses of the sanctuary above, since the Holy Temple below draws power from the sanctuary above, which is called Zevul. And therefore Solomon, peace be upon him, said (1 Kings 8:13): ״I have surely built You a house of habitation (זבול),״ and this is the secret of the verse (Judges 5:14): ״ומזבולון מושכים בשבט סופר״ — 'and from Zebulun those who draw with the staff of the scribe,' for there are those who draw downward from Zebulun, and what is drawn is Zebulun. And from this it may further be understood that from Zebulun they draw, and what is drawn is סופר (sofer), which is from the language of counting (ספירה), and the intention is regarding the first one — and understand this. And just as it is written in the creation of the world (Genesis 2:4): ״ביום עשות ה׳ אלהים,״ so in the Mishkan you find correspondingly (Numbers 7:1): ״ויהי ביום כלות משה״ — 'and it came to pass on the day Moses finished.' And just as it is written in the creation of the world (Genesis 1:31): ״וירא אלהים את כל אשר עשה והנה טוב מאד״ — 'and Elohim saw all that He had made, and behold, it was very good,' so you find in the Mishkan (Exodus 39:43): ״וירא משה את כל המלאכה והנה עשו אותה״ — 'and Moses saw all the work, and behold, they had done it.' And just as the work of creation of the world did not override the Sabbath, as it is stated (Genesis 2:2): ״וישבות ביום השביעי״ — 'and He rested on the seventh day,' so in the Mishkan, the work of the Mishkan did not override the Sabbath, as it is stated (Exodus 35:2): ״ששת ימים תעשה מלאכה״ — 'six days shall work be done.' And just as the world was created and is destined to be annulled and renewed after the Sabbath, so the Mishkan and the Temple are destined to be annulled and destroyed and renewed. And just as we find in the creation of the world (Genesis 2:16): ״ויצו ה׳ אלהים את האדם לאמר״ — 'and HaShem Elohim commanded the human, saying,' so you find in the Mishkan (Exodus 36:6): ״ויצו משה ויעבירו קול במחנה לאמר״ — 'and Moses commanded, and they caused a proclamation to pass through the camp, saying.' And just as we find in the creation of the world that the Holy One, blessed be He, was called Shaddai, because He said to His world די — 'enough,' correspondingly you find in the Mishkan (ibid. 7): ״והמלאכה היתה דים״ — 'and the material was sufficient'; this is the work above, which by way of the Kabbalah is called the work of the heavens and the earth." "<b>And in Pirkei.</b> In Pirkei Rabbi Eliezer you will find why His name is called El Shaddai: for He said to the heavens, 'Enough (dai),' and they stood, because they were stretching and going onward until He said to them, 'Enough.' And just as in the creation of the world there are heaven and earth, which are witnesses to Israel in the keeping of the Torah, as it is said (Deuteronomy 30:19): ״העידותי בכם היום את השמים ואת הארץ״ — so too in the Tabernacle: 'the Tabernacle of the Testimony.' Just as in the creation of the world there were seven days of Genesis, so too in the Tabernacle: seven days of consecration. And just as in the creation of the world אלהים appears thirty-two times, so too in the Tabernacle He filled them with wisdom of thirty-two. And there is no doubt that there are other things besides these that I have mentioned but have not written, some of which I too know, and some of which I do not know as well; and this particular detail that I have mentioned and innovated is a doorway to understanding the rest of the matter." "<b>And furthermore.</b> They said in the Midrash: ״אלה פקודי המשכן משכן״ — why is משכן משכן written twice? Because the Temple is destined to be taken as collateral (להתמשכן) twice: the First Temple and the Second Temple, as it is said (Nehemiah 1:7): ״חבל חבלנו לך״ — end of quotation. And if you apply your mind to the phrase המשכן משכן העדות, you will find in them an allusion to the number of years that the First Temple and the Second Temple stood, and likewise from the exodus from Egypt until Solomon built the Temple: for המשכן in its numerical value with its letters alludes to the Second Temple, and so משכן in its numerical value alludes to the First Temple, and so העדת is deficient in its waw and its reckoning is four hundred and eighty years from when they went out from Egypt until Solomon built the Temple; and the reason it falls short by one of the reckoning is that in the second year after their departure the Tabernacle was built. And all these calculations Moses saw by the holy spirit, and this is: ״אלה פקודי המשכן משכן העדות.״ Another interpretation: משכן העדות — Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai said: there is no 'testimony' except Torah, as it is said (Deuteronomy 4:45): ״אלה העדות והחקים והמשפטים״. A parable: a king who had a daughter, and built her a palace and seated her behind seven curtains; he said: whoever enters upon my daughter as it were enters upon my honor. Thus two names are written for the Tabernacle: one says משכן העדות — this is Torah, and one says ״לפני משכן ה׳״. Said the Holy One, blessed be He: whoever disgraces such a house as it were disgraces My honor. You may know this, for when Titus (Adrianus) entered the Temple, he would blaspheme and revile. David said: Master of the universe! (Psalms 74:5) ״יודע כמביא למעלה בסבך עץ״ — ״קרדומות״ — they brought upon them as if they were going up to cut cedars and make ladders to ascend to the firmament, but they cannot, so they leave them and come upon us, as it is said (there, 74:7): ״באו גוים בנחלתך״. Rabbi Chiya bar Abba said: Why are the idolatrous nations compared to a man who hated the minister and sought to quarrel with him but could not? What did he do? He went to the theater that was under him and sought to topple it, and he was afraid of the minister lest he kill him. He took an iron claw and placed it underneath it, saying: since I am toppling the foundation, the theater falls. Thus said the idolatrous nations concerning Israel, as it is said (there, 2:2): ״יתיצבו מלכי ארץ ורוזנים נוסדו יחד על ה׳ ועל משיחו״. A parable: one who cannot strike the donkey strikes the saddle. A parable: one who cannot strike the lion strikes the donkey."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {5 "The opening 'After he instructed…' indicates this comment is part of a proem continuing from a preceding discussion (likely from Mishlei/Proverbs). The proem verse quoted is Proverbs 15:17. The citation '(שמות ל״ב:י״א)' for Exodus 32:11 is written as given; the phrase למה ה׳ יחרה אפך is from Exodus 32:11 (with י׳ standing for the divine name). The final words 'ונתרצו באלה' trail off, completing the wordplay from Exodus 38:21, cited above in the lemma."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:09:07.273864353Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}